E2FE                             ; The checked build works the new top out first, in T0 and Y, and stops with
E2FE                             ; H, the count still on the stack, if it is at or past the limit, below the
E2FE                             ; program's end, or if the 16-bit add wrapped. A wrap is the V flag of the
E2FE                             ; high bytes" add with the old top"s sign bit flipped: that makes the old top
E2FE                             ; a signed number from -8000h, so the add overflows exactly when the true
E2FE                             ; sum leaves 0000h-FFFFh.
